{"entries":{"chapter-72-study-ethical-issues|Chapter 72 Study: Ethical Issues":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Ethical dilemmas involve uncertainty or disagreement about the right action, often because stakeholders hold conflicting values (p1097–1098).":["u3"],"copy|Clarify whether the issue is ethical, legal or another kind of problem; assemble medical facts, patient preferences, others' perspectives and relevant ethics knowledge (p1098).":["u4"],"copy|Do not assume values from age, diagnosis or background. Shared decisions combine the patient's goals with clinician information and recommendations (p1098).":["u5"],"copy|Capacity is assessed for the specific decision. A capacitated patient may choose an option the team or family considers unsafe (p1097, p1099).":["u6"],"copy|When a patient chooses home despite risk, explore support and risk reduction rather than treating disagreement as incapacity (p1099).":["u7"],"copy|If capacity is lacking, involve the appropriate surrogate. Known wishes guide substituted judgment; when wishes are unknown, weigh benefits, burdens and risks in the patient's best interests (p1102–1103).":["u8"],"copy|Surrogate decision-making can be stressful; recommendations should help the surrogate represent the patient rather than merely choose from an unexplained menu (p1098, p1102–1103).":["u9"],"copy|Offer the opportunity to receive diagnostic information, while asking how much the patient wants to know. A family's request for nondisclosure should prompt exploration of the patient's preferences (p1101).":["u10"],"copy|Withholding and withdrawing life-prolonging treatment are ethically equivalent, though withdrawal may be psychologically harder (p1103).":["u11"],"copy|Palliative sedation is a last-resort response to refractory distress in terminal illness with comfort-focused goals, after interdisciplinary evaluation (p1105).":["u12"],"copy|Sedation should be proportionate to the duration of symptoms and not expected to shorten life; existential suffering alone remains controversial and needs specialist assessment (p1105).":["u13"],"copy|Driving concerns involve confidentiality, patient safety and public safety; the chapter notes that reporting obligations vary by US state (p1098).":["u14"],"copy|Moral distress occurs when clinicians feel compelled to provide care they believe wrong; unresolved conflict can lead to burnout (p1097).":["u15"],"copy|Under resource scarcity, use transparent allocation principles and triage committees; the chapter rejects excluding patients solely by age (p1098–1109).":["u16"],"copy|Dementia does not automatically settle capacity, disclosure preferences or treatment goals; these require individualized assessment (p1099, p1101–1103).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q67":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 72s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 72 - Ethical Issues; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Recognizing ethical conflict":["u22"],"copy|Capacity & surrogate decisions":["u23"],"copy|Disclosure & end-of-life care":["u24"],"copy|Safety, systems & exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u26"],"copy|Eight past-exam questions link to this chapter by printed citation or related teaching.":["u26"],"copy|In the chapter’s US ethics framework, withholding and withdrawing unwanted life-prolonging treatment are ethically equivalent, though withdrawal may be psychologically harder. Israeli law separately restricts stopping continuous treatment when stopping could cause death (p1103 and Israeli Dying Patient Law, §21).":["u11"]},"maxId":26}
